WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.968 --> 00:00:03.872
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.529 --> 00:00:10.695
KATHERINE MILLER: Hi, Craig.

00:00:10.695 --> 00:00:13.153
Good to see you here today at
Google Cloud Next-- hopefully

00:00:13.153 --> 00:00:14.440
a good event for you so far.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:15.240
CRAIG BOX: It's been fun.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.329
KATHERINE MILLER:
Excellent, excellent.

00:00:16.329 --> 00:00:18.400
So Craig, can you tell
me a little bit today

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.290
about GKE Hybrid Orchestra?

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:21.520
CRAIG BOX: Yes, of course.

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:24.010
We've this week launched
the Cloud Services Platform,

00:00:24.010 --> 00:00:25.660
which has a number
of components that

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:28.300
make it possible to build
a hybrid infrastructure

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.980
across Google Cloud in
on-premises environments.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:33.160
We have GKE On-prem,
which allows

00:00:33.160 --> 00:00:35.440
you to run GKE,
managed by Google,

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:37.060
in your own data center.

00:00:37.060 --> 00:00:40.570
We have Istio 1.0, which allows
us to build a hybrid service

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:43.480
mesh that connects the two
networks together and allows

00:00:43.480 --> 00:00:46.070
us to seamlessly route traffic
between the two locations.

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:47.236
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:00:47.236 --> 00:00:49.540
So when we interact
with this demo,

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:53.170
if I slide these
back and forth, what

00:00:53.170 --> 00:00:55.270
will we be creating with this?

00:00:55.270 --> 00:00:56.909
CRAIG BOX: We're
creating an orchestra.

00:00:56.909 --> 00:00:58.450
That's the metaphor
we're using here.

00:00:58.450 --> 00:01:01.510
And it's great, because we talk
about orchestration with GKE.

00:01:01.510 --> 00:01:04.569
And we have a piece of music
which we are piping through

00:01:04.569 --> 00:01:05.590
in many formats.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.850
We're sending that out as
HTTP calls, REST calls,

00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:11.710
to our Instrument Services.

00:01:11.710 --> 00:01:13.870
When they are
received, the music

00:01:13.870 --> 00:01:16.480
is tagged with the instrument
that it needs to be played,

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:18.430
and then that's
written to Firebase.

00:01:18.430 --> 00:01:19.840
We have those
Instrument Services

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:22.830
running in deployments in
both our GKE Cluster and GKE

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:24.010
On-prem.

00:01:24.010 --> 00:01:27.250
And then the wall you see here
is showing both a visualization

00:01:27.250 --> 00:01:28.720
of the scale of
the pods that are

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.190
required for the instrument.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:31.930
So again, if our
bassoon is playing--

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:34.210
has five units of
traffic-- we can

00:01:34.210 --> 00:01:36.340
see there's five
bassoons here playing it.

00:01:36.340 --> 00:01:38.860
And you can also see
and hear the music.

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.930
And in this case, you
can see, the music

00:01:40.930 --> 00:01:42.734
is being redirected
so all the parts are

00:01:42.734 --> 00:01:45.150
being played by our on-premises
environment on the bottom.

00:01:45.150 --> 00:01:45.620
KATHERINE MILLER: I see.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:47.703
CRAIG BOX: So we can see
the overlap of the colors

00:01:47.703 --> 00:01:50.260
as the instrument
music is being played--

00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.799
as the redirects are causing
the music to be played--

00:01:52.799 --> 00:01:54.340
by those four
instruments down there.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:56.560
What you see here
is a service which

00:01:56.560 --> 00:02:00.430
runs on Google Cloud, which
generates music and pushes it

00:02:00.430 --> 00:02:02.680
through various instrument
services that run

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.182
in our MicroServices cluster.

00:02:04.182 --> 00:02:05.890
We have some of those
instruments running

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.690
on Google Cloud in
the same cluster

00:02:07.690 --> 00:02:10.307
as the conductor that's
making the music happen.

00:02:10.307 --> 00:02:12.640
And then we also have some
of those instruments deployed

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.500
in our on-premises environment.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:17.049
Using Istio in its
multi-cluster mode,

00:02:17.049 --> 00:02:19.090
we're able to connect
those two clusters together

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:20.920
to the same network
control plane.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:24.790
So we're able to get traffic
sent to services based

00:02:24.790 --> 00:02:26.530
on the hostname, and
it doesn't matter

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:29.860
if that service is deployed
in the Cloud cluster on GKE

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:32.707
or on-premise with GKE On-prem.

00:02:32.707 --> 00:02:34.540
You can address that
traffic, and it will be

00:02:34.540 --> 00:02:35.990
received by the right service.

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:37.810
Another great
feature of Istio is

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:39.640
to do traffic routing,
which you might

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:41.990
do to test a new
service by saying,

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:44.440
I'll deploy the new service
and direct a small percentage

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:45.357
of traffic to it.

00:02:45.357 --> 00:02:47.440
And once I'm comfortable
that there's no failures,

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.360
gradually ramp it up
over time until I'm

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.810
sending 100% of my traffic,
and then turn my old one off.

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:55.650
What we have in this environment
is these little patch cords,

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.810
where we're able to say,
the music for the bassoon--

00:02:58.810 --> 00:03:01.630
generally, the conductor will
give that to the bassoon player

00:03:01.630 --> 00:03:02.560
to play.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:05.960
We have scale, so we can say,
if we send five parts of that,

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:07.680
we need five bassoon players.

00:03:07.680 --> 00:03:10.660
But we can also use Istio's
virtual services to say,

00:03:10.660 --> 00:03:13.139
instead of that music going
to the bassoon player,

00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:15.430
let's have a little pixie
come and take that on the way

00:03:15.430 --> 00:03:18.070
and intercept it and give
it to the clarinet player.

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.640
So now, it says, there
are five parts of traffic

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:22.900
that need to be played
by the clarinet player.

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:24.960
Scale the clarinet up to five.

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.560
We don't need any bassoons, so
we can scale them back down.

00:03:27.560 --> 00:03:29.470
And we can transparently
redirect that

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:31.562
without the calling or
the calling application

00:03:31.562 --> 00:03:33.520
having to know anything
about what's happening.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:34.520
KATHERINE MILLER: I see.

00:03:34.520 --> 00:03:36.509
And what song is this
orchestra playing?

00:03:36.509 --> 00:03:38.050
CRAIG BOX: If you
don't recognize it,

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:39.310
this is the theme from Tetris.

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:40.240
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:42.323
CRAIG BOX: And actually,
it's a Russian folk song,

00:03:42.323 --> 00:03:45.385
I believe from the 1890s, which
was made popular in the 1980s.

00:03:45.385 --> 00:03:47.770
So a nice bit of symmetry
there, and a nice little bit

00:03:47.770 --> 00:03:50.270
of interplay with the concept
of Kubernetes container

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:50.770
scheduling.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.000
We'll often Tetris as
a metaphor for how we

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:54.430
slot all the pieces into place.

00:03:54.430 --> 00:03:55.596
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:03:55.596 --> 00:03:58.030
And what makes this an
interesting problem for us

00:03:58.030 --> 00:04:00.100
to be solving here at Next?

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:02.500
CRAIG BOX: Kubernetes has
become the API of choice

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.649
for deploying workloads,
especially workloads that

00:04:04.649 --> 00:04:06.190
are containerized,
or things that can

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:07.840
build on top of that metaphor.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.660
And we have had
GKE for some time.

00:04:09.660 --> 00:04:11.770
It's been generally available
for three years now.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:13.853
But if you want to run it
in your own data center,

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.510
it's either take the
open source parts

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.589
or take some third
party product, which

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:21.070
generally embeds Kubernetes
but isn't the same as the GKE

00:04:21.070 --> 00:04:21.910
environment.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:23.660
They're all conformant,
so we know that it

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:25.046
will run the same workloads.

00:04:25.046 --> 00:04:26.920
But the management is
different, the versions

00:04:26.920 --> 00:04:28.240
and the upgrade cycle--
you have to look

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:29.020
at a lot of that yourself.

00:04:29.020 --> 00:04:29.650
KATHERINE MILLER: I see.

00:04:29.650 --> 00:04:31.180
CRAIG BOX: So first
of all, the problem

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.596
of running GKE in
your data center

00:04:32.596 --> 00:04:34.420
can be solved by GKE On-prem.

00:04:34.420 --> 00:04:36.294
You can use the same
management console.

00:04:36.294 --> 00:04:37.960
All of the things
that are running here,

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.120
we can see on the screen
we have over here.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:41.890
But we're able to
see the workloads

00:04:41.890 --> 00:04:43.480
in the single pane of glass--

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.814
Cloud and on-prem together.

00:04:45.814 --> 00:04:47.980
The second thing that's
been complicated in the past

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.800
is meshing these
two things together.

00:04:49.800 --> 00:04:52.130
It's connecting these
two disparate networks

00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:53.627
so that just by
calling a hostname,

00:04:53.627 --> 00:04:55.960
we don't need to care whether
the service is implemented

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:58.210
by this cluster or
the one on-prem.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.355
We're able just to
say, call the bassoon.

00:05:00.355 --> 00:05:01.329
Send it to those pods.

00:05:01.329 --> 00:05:03.120
It will be served
wherever they're running.

00:05:03.120 --> 00:05:05.453
And indeed, we can change the
split and move instruments

00:05:05.453 --> 00:05:06.664
between the environments.

00:05:06.664 --> 00:05:07.830
KATHERINE MILLER: Excellent.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.510
Craig, thank you so much
for your time today.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:09.610
CRAIG BOX: Thank you.

00:05:09.610 --> 00:05:11.170
KATHERINE MILLER: And enjoy
the last day of the conference.

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.086
CRAIG BOX: Absolutely.

00:05:12.086 --> 00:05:14.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

